{"conversation_evidence":{"admissible_pre_tfg_turns":[{"create_time":1690183079.369699,"event_index":2,"role":"user","source_node_id":"aaa2d4f9-2c62-4d4d-a3b5-234d1295f93d","source_parent_id":"78bc391f-9bda-428a-b16a-4379821135ac","source_record_index":2,"text":"You are a Python expert.\nHow can I create a deep copy of a variable?","turn_id":"turn_000002"}],"first_generation":{"artifacts":[{"candidate_id":"GTC_000003_000270_000607_899ef9a484a88e8c","completeness":"complete","container":"markdown_fence","continuation_status":"none","detector":{"name":"generated-technical-content-v2","version":"2"},"detector_evidence":["complete_standalone_markdown_fence","import_or_include","call_expression"],"end_offset":607,"fence_language":"python","kind":"source_code","legacy_artifact_id":"ARTIFACT_000003_001","order_within_response":1,"raw_text":"import copy\n\noriginal_list = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\ndeep_copied_list = copy.deepcopy(original_list)\n\n# Now, if you modify the nested list inside the original list...\noriginal_list[0][0] = 99\n\nprint(original_list)        # Outputs: [[99, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\nprint(deep_copied_list)     # Outputs: [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]] (remains unchanged)\n","raw_text_sha256":"899ef9a484a88e8cb83969d2e4691cf937efc7f97c9463b2969548dd6de8d51e","source_event_index":3,"source_record_index":3,"source_response_id":"turn_000003","source_turn_text_sha256":"641f08d0b3371069a41ea5be398d0bb35731bd4075032ce8a6c70e19bf8cb0ce","start_offset":270,"status":"candidate_requires_semantic_review"}],"identification":{"ambiguity_reason":"","artifact_refs":["GTC_000003_000270_000607_899ef9a484a88e8c"],"boundary":"exclusive_before:turn_000003","candidate_artifact_ids":["GTC_000003_000270_000607_899ef9a484a88e8c"],"family_id":"FGF_768933aaba0fe765a583e905","family_id_provenance":{"algorithm":"sha256","canonical_input":{"artifact_ids":["GTC_000003_000270_000607_899ef9a484a88e8c"],"case_id":"CASE_179B36843FBE"},"canonicalization":"json-sort-keys-compact-utf8","case_id_included":true,"digest_hex":"768933aaba0fe765a583e905de1de2133701a3112bb1eefe6647aed00be028ab","digest_prefix_length":24},"family_label":"Python deep-copy example using copy.deepcopy","rationale":"The sole candidate is a complete Python example generated in the first assistant response, directly triggered by the user's request for creating a deep copy.","response_event_index":3,"response_turn_id":"turn_000003","status":"complete","target_prompt_event_index":2,"target_prompt_id":"turn_000002"}},"first_generation_response":{"create_time":1690183097.145534,"event_index":3,"role":"assistant","source_node_id":"1399cfcb-349c-4316-a9d6-9919af41d413","source_parent_id":"aaa2d4f9-2c62-4d4d-a3b5-234d1295f93d","source_record_index":3,"text":"Certainly! In Python, if you want to create a deep copy of a variable, especially for complex objects like lists or dictionaries that contain nested objects, you should use the `copy` module which provides the `deepcopy` function.\n\nHere's how you can use it:\n\n```python\nimport copy\n\noriginal_list = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\ndeep_copied_list = copy.deepcopy(original_list)\n\n# Now, if you modify the nested list inside the original list...\noriginal_list[0][0] = 99\n\nprint(original_list)        # Outputs: [[99, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\nprint(deep_copied_list)     # Outputs: [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]] (remains unchanged)\n```\n\nThe deep copy creates a new object that's a copy of the original, but recursively creates new objects for any mutable objects found inside the original. This ensures that changes to the original object or any of its nested objects won't affect the deep copied object.\n\nOn the other hand, if you used a shallow copy (e.g., using `copy.copy()` or for lists, simply doing `copied_list = original_list[:]`), changes to nested objects inside the original would reflect in the copied object as well.","turn_id":"turn_000003"},"target_developer_prompt":{"create_time":1690183079.369699,"event_index":2,"role":"user","source_node_id":"aaa2d4f